    "summary": "Classic '66 Fillmore set with stellar In The Pines and Cream Puff War",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a strong folk‑blues focus with standout performances of In The Pines, Cream Puff War and Viola Lee Blues. Jerry’s Gibson tone and Pigpen’s organ drive the energy while Phil provides a solid bass foundation. Charlie Miller’s soundboard transfer is pristine, giving crystal‑clear vocals and balanced instrumentation.",
    "review": "On July 16, 1966 the Grateful Dead took the Fillmore Auditorium by storm, opening a set that leaned heavily into folk and blues before launching into their trademark psychedelic jams. Early numbers like “I Know You Rider” and “Big Boss Man” set a loose vibe, but it’s the mid‑set gems – a haunting “In The Pines,” the rousing “Cream Puff War,” and the blistering “Viola Lee Blues” – that truly define this performance. Pigpen’s organ roars through “Good Morning Little School Girl” and “Standing On The Corner,” giving the show a surf‑rock edge, while Jerry’s Gibson tone and vocal clarity shine on every cut. Phil’s bass underpins the whole thing with a “crank‑Marshall‑ish” growl that keeps the groove solid. Despite minor edits to the opening/closing tunes and a brief mic drop on “Cardboard Cowboy,” the recording captured by Charlie Miller is crystal clear, making this one of the most vivid early‑era documents available.",
